1 NOTE {geni:about_me} CARL JOHAN HANSSON BREHM OLIVECRONA
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 26 October 1854, the son of Hans August Paulus Olivecrona and Charlotta Lofqvist. Both of his parents died when Carl was very young, his father dying when he was 6 years old. Hans August Olivecrona cared very much for his son
2 CONC  and he asked his younger brother Knut (S.R.D.K. Olivecrona, who was 11 years younger than Hans), to look after Carl when he died and he left 300 pounds to Carl in his will. Carl's uncle Knut did indeed look after him and became h
2 CONC is Guardian. As a boy, Carl was known as Carl Johan Hansson
2 CONT  and he lived with and was looked after by a Mrs Lychou. She and Knut Olivecrona were responsible for Carl receiving a very good education (Carl could for instance, speak six  languages fluently). 
2 CONT
2 CONT  On 10 June 1871, Knut Olivecrona visited Mrs Lychou to arrange for Carl to go to sea, to perhaps follow in his father's footsteps. Carl was 17 years old at that time. After eight years, Carl left the sea and he worked for a merch
2 CONC ant in Stockholm who was at that time, the British Vice Consul. Carl later operated his own business in Stockholm in the oil trade and on 24 October 1885, he was appointed Royal British Vice Consul in Stockholm. At that time he ha
2 CONC d the name Carl Johan Hansson Brehm. Carl continued to have a close relationship with his uncles and cousins and he visited often and was usually invited to family parties. He was described by the Olivecrona family as a nice perso
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In 1889, Carl changed his surname to Olivecrona, his natural father's name. He was assisted in this by his uncle, S. R. D. Knut Olivecrona who, in order to officially establish his parentage, arranged for Carl to be listed in Gab
2 CONC riel Anrep's 1890 Calendar of Swedish Nobles, where on page 688, he is listed as the son of Hans August Paulus Olivecrona and Charlotta Löfqvist . 
2 CONT
2 CONT  In 1854 he left his positon as British Vice Consul and emigrated to New Zealand, where he became Charles John Olivecrona to merge more easily into the New Zealand society. He went into a business partnership with H. E. McDonald a
2 CONC s wool merchants but also dealing in oil products.
2 CONT
2 CONT  On 2/6/1897 in Nelson, he married Flora Cooper McDonald. They had five children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Eric Edward Detlof born 17/6/1898 at Belmont Nena Elisa born 6/3/1900 at Blenheim
2 CONT
2 CONT Cecil Henri August born 16/4/1901 at Blenheim
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Malcolm Bartlett born 23/1/1903 at Lower Hutt
2 CONT
2 CONT Elsa Kathleen born 10/4/1908 at Christchurch
2 CONT
2 CONT Flora Olivecrona died at Lower Hutt on 4th October 1912 aged 35 years , from a thyroid problem which was not so readily treated in those days.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following this, the family moved to and grew up on a farm in Levin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles John Olivecrona died at Levin on 3th December 1939. In his last years he suffered from glaucoma and had become blind. During those years he lived with and was looked after by his daughter Nena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.igrin.co.nz/~hotchoc/CJolive.htm
