1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Carl was born in Fredrickshald, Norway and was an undergardener to the King of Norway for some time.  Was given a reference by the head gardener, Carl Kaiser of the palace gardens in Christiania, as the capital of 
2 CONC Norway was called at the time.  Whereabouts of the document is unknown.  It was written in Norwegian but beneath that was an English translation by the Britich Chaplain in Chritiania.  There is a red seal on the original document,
2 CONC  of the King of Norway. It tells of Carl's ability and 'indefatigable industry' and recommends him most strongly to all fellow gardeners He arrived in Australia by immigrant ship the SS Humboldt at Bowen Queensland on August 4, 18
2 CONC 72. He worked for a time in the Sydney Botanical Gardens. He then answered an advertisement by Captain Ogilvie for a head gardener at Yulgilbar Castle near Grafton. He was then employed at Yulgilbar Castole and it was there that h
2 CONC e met his wife Christina McFarlane November 1874.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Carl was born in Fredrickshald, Norway and was an undergardener to the King of Norway for some time. Was given a reference by the head gardener, Carl Kaiser of the palace gardens in Christiania, as the capital of Norway was called
2 CONC  at the time. Whereaboutsof the document is unknown. It was written in Norwegian but beneath that was an English translation by the Britich Chaplain in Chritiania. There is a red seal on the original document, of the King of Norwa
2 CONC y. It tells of Carl's ability and 'indefatigable industry' and recommends him most strongly to all fellow gardeners He arrived in Australia by immigrant ship the SS Humboldt at Bowen Queensland on August 4, 1872. He worked for a t
2 CONC ime in the Sydney Botanical Gardens. He then answered an advertisement by Captain Ogilvie for a head gardener at Yulgilbar Castle near Grafton. He was then employed at Yulgilbar Castole and it was there that he met his wife Christ
2 CONC ina McFarlane November 1874.
